            "creation_time": "2026-11-26T01:45:21Z",
            "text": "## Summary\n\nWith integer or double subscripts and more than one replacement value, `x[i] <- y` runs 1.2 to 1.4 times as fast in benchmarks on 10,000-element vectors. Enhancement: the patch preserves assignment behavior.\n\n## Details\n\n`integerSubscript()` and `realSubscript()` already check for `NA` while processing the subscript, but do not pass the result on. When `y` has more than one element, `VectorAssign()` in `src/main/subassign.c` scans the index again.\n\nThe patch adds `makeSubscriptNA()` to pass the result to `VectorAssign()`. If the index is known to contain no `NA`, `VectorAssign()` skips the second scan. Character subscripts skip it too: `stringSubscript()` makes unmatched names, `NA` included, new elements. It still scans logical subscripts, whose code bug 19364 rewrites. Other callers continue to use `makeSubscript()`, which now wraps `makeSubscriptNA()`.\n\nIn the attached benchmark, `subassign-na-check-bench.R`, the time per element for `x[idx] <- y` (in place) on 10,000-element vectors fell from 1.93 to 1.43 ns with Apple clang (arm64) and from 1.95 to 1.53 ns with GCC 15.2 (x86_64). Timings for logical and character subscripts and for single replacement values were unchanged.\n\nThe attached patch, `subassign-na-check.diff`, is based on r90991 and includes a regression test covering `NA`, `NaN`, and infinite subscripts. `make check` passes on macOS arm64, and CI passes on Linux (x86_64 and arm64, GCC and clang), macOS and Windows. CI results and additional timings are available at https://github.com/r-devel/r-svn/pull/541. The patch for bug 19367 also changes the `makeSubscript()` call in `VectorAssign()`, so whichever of the two is applied second needs that line merged by hand.\n\n## AI Disclosure\n\nThis patch was prepared with the assistance of Claude Code. The issue was discovered while reviewing the loops in `VectorAssign()` for performance.",
